  "strategy": "1. **Approach and Rationale**:\n   - We adopt a multi-stage optimization strategy combining coarse-to-fine resolution and population-based search to escape local optima.\n   - Start with a low-resolution parent (70 samples) optimized on [0,1], then refine it at 280-point resolution to exploit structural improvements.\n   - Use a patchwork optimization: random walks of local parents, repair mechanisms to ensure feasible solutions, and dimensionality reduction via tensor train factorization to reduce computation.\n   - The key bottleneck addressed is the high dimensionality of 280 variables; we mitigate this through coarse exploration and dimensionality reduction.\n\n2. **Mathematical Specification**:\n   - Variables: Represented as tensor train factors of size 70 with mode ranks [50, 14, 3]\n   - Objective: min(max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx)\n   - Constraints: 0\u2264h[i]\u22641 and \u222bh(x)dx=1 enforced by projection\n   - Tensor Train: `h = TT(B1, B2, ..., Bk)` with cores B\u1d62 \u2208 \u211d\u207f\u1d48\u207f\u1d48, s.t. contracted product equals h\n   - Repair: Clip and rescale after perturbations to maintain integral condition\n\n3. **Implementation Procedure**:\n   - **Phase 0**: Validate input resolution and parent solution\n   - **Phase 1**: Coarse optimization (140-point tensor train)\n     - Downsample 280 to 140 points\n     - Use SLSQP with bounds [0,1] and integral=70\n     - Repair violated values by clipping then scaling\n   - **Phase 2**: Refinement (70-point optimization)\n     - Select best coarse solution\n     - Adapt tensor train representation for 70 variables\n     - Use Bayesian optimization with random forest surrogate\n   - **Phase 3**: Final adjustment\n     - Restore full 280-point resolution via tensor expansion\n     - Verify integral and bounds via tensor-train-based projection\n\n4. **Search Choices**:\n   - Starting tensor train rank = 14\n   - Random walk \u03c3-value = 0.025\n   - Model surrogate for 20 initial evaluations\n   - Optimize with n_candidates=250 with equidistant random seeds\n   - Repair window size=5% of samples\n\n5. **Budget & Robustness**:\n   - Primary cost: 280-point correlation ~15k operations\n   - Backup plans: cache evals; use fallback parent for timeouts\n   - Total eval operations estimates: ~80k ops (6.6s per eval)\n   - Recovery: Linear scaling between coarse evaluation C\u2080 and rejected samples if flag=True\n   - Time estimation: 9 configs * 12 evals ~ 14s\n   - Budget check: Time-based stopping during Bayesian optimization\n\n6. **Validation Contract**:\n   - Integral: |sum(h\u00b2)\u00b7dx| = 0.6% deviation maximum\n   - Bounds: Using concave-repair ensures 0\u2264h\u22650\n   - Correlation recomputation with floating-point guard\n   - Final clean-up ensures [0,1] tolerance <1e-6",
